#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""cst-vm: copies of a libvirt VM (disks, UEFI variables, TPM state) taken
before a risky change, such as a Fedora major upgrade. It is the program
behind `cst vm` (vm-setup.sh).

Usage:
  backup [VM] [--full]   (root) copy the VM while it is shut off (not
                         saved; checked again after the copy):
                         every file disk, the UEFI variables (NVRAM), the
                         software TPM state, the definition (XML) and a
                         manifest, to
                         /var/lib/libvirt/cst-backups/VM/YYYYmmddTHHMMSSZ/.
                         Default: reflink copies (instant on btrfs; they
                         share blocks with the originals, so they protect
                         against a repair tool or an upgrade writing to the
                         image, NOT against losing the disks or the
                         filesystem). --full: real copies (needs free space
                         for the allocated size). Prints how to restore;
                         never restores by itself
  list [VM]              the backups, from /var/lib/cst/vm-backups.json
                         (readable by every user)
  status [VM]            the VM's state, its CPU mode and whether the CET/MBEC
                         features are disabled, whether its disks and network
                         card match `tune`, the last backup, whether the
                         virtualization packages changed since then, and the
                         NOCOW state of the VM image folders
  tune plan [VM] [--guest-has-virtio]
                         the changes to the definition that give the VM the
                         recommended virtual hardware: disks on virtio-scsi,
                         cache='none' io='native' discard='unmap'
                         detect_zeroes='unmap', network card virtio (same
                         MAC). For a Windows guest (or one not known to be
                         Linux), the bus and network card changes need
                         --guest-has-virtio: the virtio drivers must be in
                         the guest first. Changes nothing
  tune apply [VM] [--guest-has-virtio]
                         (root) the same, asks, saves the old definition and
                         runs `virsh define`: effective at the VM's next start
  nocow plan [--root]    the VM image folders (libvirt system and session,
                         GNOME Boxes RPM and Flatpak): btrfs, NOCOW mark (+C),
                         and the images in them still copy-on-write, with the
                         commands to convert each. --root: through sudo, to
                         read the root-only system folder. Changes nothing
  nocow apply            sets +C on those folders (sudo for the system one;
                         creates /var/lib/libvirt/images when libvirt is
                         installed). Never converts an existing image
  help

VM defaults to VM_NAME in /etc/cpu-setup/cpu-setup.conf (win11-devbox).
Settings (all optional, default yes): /etc/cst/vm.conf with
VM_NOCOW, VM_TUNE_DISK_BUS, VM_TUNE_DISK_DRIVER, VM_TUNE_NIC (yes or no).
Exit status: 0 fine, 1 refused or failed, 2 usage.
"""

